  - [1mReturn the userId from [38;5;153mlogin()[0m — the function (today a stub, later the real API response) is what establishes
    identity, so it hands back [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m and the caller logs it. Buys: the id is actually correct, and
    it keeps working when the stub becomes a real POST that returns a server-side id. Costs: nothing tracked on a [3mfailed
[0m    login, since there's no id to return. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd a caller-supplied tracking id parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, trackingId)[39m where the caller generates a
    per-attempt id (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m). Buys: failed and successful attempts are both traceable, and it
    correlates across retries. Costs: it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not the person — if you wanted the person, this doesn't
    give it to you. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd [38;5;153muserId[39m as a literal parameter, as asked[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m. Buys: exactly what you described.
    Costs: today the only value the caller could pass is [38;5;153musername[39m, which [38;5;153mlogin()[39m already receives — so it's a duplicated
    argument until something upstream actually knows an id. Hardest to undo, because it's the one that bakes a wrong
    assumption into the signature.

  My recommendation is returning the userId. It's the only option where the value logged is reliably the right one, and
  it survives the stub becoming a real API call. I'd pick the tracking-id parameter instead only if the thing you
  actually want is failed-attempt visibility.

